Exothermic continuous stirred tank reactors are often said to be hard to control because they may operate at open-loop unstable steady states, yet the instability, the actuator limits and the loss of heat-transfer capacity are rarely separated. This work separates them on a three-state reactor--jacket model with a coolant valve limited in amplitude and slew rate. With the nominal feed the heat balance has one stable root for every admissible coolant flow, so an unstable steady state exists only when the feed is cooled; at $T_f=\TfSad$~K the middle branch at $\TSad$~K is a saddle with $\lambda_1=+\lamASad$~min$^{-1}$. A single-loop SIMC controller stabilizes this saddle only for closed-loop time constants below $\tcMaxPI$~min, whereas the classical bound $\tau_c<\tau_u-\theta$ is exact only for proportional action. A sequential cascade works because tight jacket regulation leaves the stable concentration--temperature sub-block; the open-loop gain ratio used to tune the master loop then has the wrong sign. The SIMC single loop is a weak baseline ($M_s=\msSimc$): against a PI optimized under $M_s\le\msMax$, the default-SIMC cascade does \emph{not} reduce the integral absolute error after a $+10$~K coolant shock over a fixed $\horizonShockWin$~min window ($\iaeTjfCascade$ versus $\iaeTjfSisoopt$~K\,min), whereas static utility feedforward does ($\iaeTjfCascadeff$~K\,min). Two limits of fouling must be kept apart. The analytical linear boundary---the heat-transfer capacity at which the frozen-$UA$ linearization of a fixed-gain loop loses the saddle---is $UA\approx\fstabCascade$--$\fstabSiso\%$ of the clean value for the SIMC loops, well before the thermodynamic limit at $\fcritSad\%$. It is a Hopf bifurcation, a complex pair crossing the imaginary axis while $K_ck\approx1.5>1$, not a collapse of the static loop gain; slow fouling ($\kappa_{\mathrm{foul}}=\kappaSweepLo$~L/mol) departs within $\gapSlowSiso$--$\gapSlowCascade$ points of it. The practical loss of regulation in fast transients is different: with $\kappa_{\mathrm{foul}}=\kappaFoul$~L/mol the single loop leaves the band at $\uaRunPctSadSiso\%$ of the clean capacity. The $M_s$-constrained PI holds the saddle down to $\fstabSisoopt\%$, and scheduling the slave gain on $1/(T_j-T_{jf})$ moves the cascade boundary only from $\fstabCascade\%$ to $\fstabCascadegs\%$, because the cascade is lost through its master-loop gain.
